Transient Global Amnesia
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Sudden-onset dense anterograde amnesia | Fully preserved personal identity and basic consciousness | Complete resolution within 24 hours

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe sudden-onset memory loss and repetitive questioning requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute ischemic stroke, intracranial hemorrhage, or complex partial status epilepticus.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and highly distressed spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden, profound cognitive deficit and confusion. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out life-threatening cerebrovascular accidents or post-ictal states, preventing irreversible brain injury prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Why are we in the living room? What day is it? (Repeated every 2 minutes by patient).
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden, profound inability to form new memories (anterograde amnesia) that began two hours ago following intense physical exertion in the garden. There is continuous, repetitive questioning about current circumstances and location. Crucially, the spouse notes a perfect preservation of immediate recall, procedural memory (patient made a cup of tea flawlessly), and completely intact personal identity and self-awareness. There is an absolute lack of epileptic features (no tonic-clonic movements, lip smacking) or clouding of consciousness. The spouse incidentally mentions the patient has a background of vague musculoskeletal aching, subtle proximal weakness, and a recent lab showing 25-OH Vitamin D <20 ng/mL, entirely unrelated to tonight's acute neurological event.
Functional Status: Physically independent, completely cognitively dependent acutely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Elopement risk due to severe disorientation to time and immediate circumstances.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient is alert, attentive, and physically comfortable, but highly perplexed and repetitively asking the same orientation questions.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination reveals an absolute absence of focal motor, sensory, or cranial nerve deficits. Pupillary responses are normal and reflexes are symmetric globally. Cerebellar testing (finger-to-nose, heel-to-shin) is perfectly intact. Gait is completely normal. Cardiovascular exam is regular without murmurs. Carotid pulses are equal without bruits.
Environmental Observations: House is secure. Patient is safely seated.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No new medications or anticholinergics identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient Global Amnesia - Status: Acute active episode.
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke (Posterior Circulation) - Ruled out clinically due to the complete absence of ataxia, diplopia, dysarthria, or focal motor/sensory weakness.

Complex Partial Seizure (Post-Ictal) - Ruled out due to the preserved level of consciousness, lack of automatisms, and absence of an initial unresponsive phase.

Hypoglycemic Encephalopathy - Ruled out as point-of-care capillary blood glucose is 112 mg/dL (6.2 mmol/L).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute cognitive deficit).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden, catastrophic loss of short-term memory mandated an immediate, high-priority after-hours assessment to exclude an acute stroke or active seizure. Telephone triage accurately identified the severe risk for a cerebrovascular event. A meticulous neurological examination confirmed an isolated anterograde amnestic syndrome without a single focal, cortical, or brainstem deficit. The perfect preservation of self-identity and procedural memory firmly established a classic presentation of Transient Global Amnesia. By recognizing this dramatic but benign neurological phenomenon and ruling out acute ischemia, the clinician immediately de-escalated the spouse's extreme panic and safely bypassed a high-stress, unnecessary emergency department stroke-call activation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided immense reassurance and psychoeducation to the family regarding the benign nature of the syndrome. The expected investigation results include a normal acute CT head with small punctate hyperintensities in the lateral hippocampus on delayed diffusion-weighted MRI, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis if formal neurology follow-up is pursued.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the patient's acute distress, write down the answers to their repetitive questions (e.g., "You are safe at home, it is Tuesday night") in large letters on a piece of paper and hand it to them; providing a constant visual anchor prevents the anxiety of a completely blank short-term memory.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: Ensure all external doors are locked and the patient is constantly supervised.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to confirm complete resolution of the amnestic episode and arrange non-urgent outpatient neuroimaging.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden headache, weakness in the face or limbs, difficulty speaking, or experiences a full-body seizure.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; acute neurological events must be closely monitored for unexpected progression.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves a complete, spontaneous resolution of the amnestic episode within 24 hours, returning the patient entirely to their baseline cognitive state.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The caregiver must seek immediate emergency medical attention if the patient loses consciousness, begins to speak with slurred words, or becomes physically weak on one side of their body.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not rushing to the hospital for an emergency brain scan because the patient's physical nerve functions are absolutely perfect; this is a temporary, harmless electrical reset in the memory center of the brain.
Actionable Steps: Do not repeatedly test the patient's memory tonight as it will only increase their frustration; speak in calm, short sentences and allow them to rest safely in a familiar chair.

